window.GG_STORIES = {"v":1,"made":"2026-10-13","k":{"v":1,"round":"s1","folder":"t"},"list":[{"id":"s002","slug":"birth-plan","title":"Birth Plan","sub":"She spent thirty years catching people on their way in.","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/birth-plan","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, Birth Plan · Read it on Grounded","mins":9,"preview":["\"Why am I still here?\" Jan cried out from her wheelchair in the corner of the dining room. She repeated herself, sharper this time, in case anyone was confused: \"Why am I still alive?\"","The room went quiet, and I could feel every eye on me, waiting to see how I'd answer.","This was my second visit with Jan. In our first visit she told me about the thirty years she spent walking babies into the world, night shifts in the labor and delivery unit, then home by morning to raise her own three kids. Nothing but good memories that day.","But a lot had changed for Jan in the last month. Her quality of life had been slipping, week by week.","Still, I was caught off guard. It was almost like she'd had the question loaded, just waiting for me to walk in.","She'd asked me the rawest question there is, straight from the heart, and I wanted to meet it with something just as honest. Not here, though. Not with the whole dining room listening. I thought fast, looking for a way to get us somewhere private.","I let my serious face crack into a grin and said, to Jan and everyone else listening, \"Well? The good Lord must have something fun planned for ya, Jan!\"","I laid it on with a southern drawl I don't usually have. Jan's whole face lit up, and a few chuckles went around the room.","I took the opening, stood, and turned her chair toward the hallway. \"Let's go talk about this, Jan. Maybe we can ask the Lord directly and figure it out.\""],"posted":"","topics":["dying"]},{"id":"s004","slug":"drift-away","title":"Drift Away","sub":"Some people don't say goodbye. They sing it.","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":true,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/drift-away","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, Drift Away · Read it on Grounded","mins":5,"preview":["Suzan had not spoken in three days.","But I knew her voice. I had been visiting her for the better part of a month, and in those early visits she had plenty to say. She told me about her husband, the long happy kind of marriage that people do not talk about enough. She told me about raising her children, about the grandchildren who came after, about the particular joy of watching your kids become parents. She had a warm and uncomplicated faith, the kind that does not need to argue with anyone. She loved God and she loved her small country church and she loved the people in it. There was nothing restless about her belief. It fit her the way a good coat fits, worn in and reliable and exactly right.","Then the cancer finished what it had started and the words stopped coming.","By then she had gone three days without food or water. She would squeeze my hand sometimes, turn her head slightly, make small gestures that told you she was still in there, still listening. But she had not opened her eyes or spoken directly to me in days. The room was dim and warm. Her walls were covered in crayon drawings and school photos and the gloriously unhinged art that only grandchildren make. More love than skill. Every one of them a small act of worship. A whole gallery of people who adored her, watching over the room she could no longer leave."],"posted":"","topics":["dying"],"full":["Suzan had not spoken in three days.","But I knew her voice. I had been visiting her for the better part of a month, and in those early visits she had plenty to say. She told me about her husband, the long happy kind of marriage that people do not talk about enough. She told me about raising her children, about the grandchildren who came after, about the particular joy of watching your kids become parents. She had a warm and uncomplicated faith, the kind that does not need to argue with anyone. She loved God and she loved her small country church and she loved the people in it. There was nothing restless about her belief. It fit her the way a good coat fits, worn in and reliable and exactly right.","Then the cancer finished what it had started and the words stopped coming.","By then she had gone three days without food or water. She would squeeze my hand sometimes, turn her head slightly, make small gestures that told you she was still in there, still listening. But she had not opened her eyes or spoken directly to me in days. The room was dim and warm. Her walls were covered in crayon drawings and school photos and the gloriously unhinged art that only grandchildren make. More love than skill. Every one of them a small act of worship. A whole gallery of people who adored her, watching over the room she could no longer leave.","That afternoon a small bluetooth speaker sat on the bedside table.","Then Dobie Gray came on. Drift Away.","The first thing I noticed was her toes.","Under the white sheet, they began to move. Left, then right. Slow and deliberate, perfectly on the beat. I stopped mid breath and just watched. After three days of stillness, Suzan was moving.","I started singing along, quietly at first, holding her hand, trying to let her feel the vibration of it. Not because I have a particularly good voice. Because it felt like the wrong moment to be shy.","What happened next I will never forget.","She lifted her chin, pointing it upward like she was looking at something above her. And then her face broke open into the widest smile I have ever seen on a dying person. Not a twitch. Not a reflex. A full, radiant, unambiguous smile that took over her whole face.","Then her shoulders started to dance.","Small movements, left and right, but completely intentional, right on the beat. She squeezed my hand as she moved and I sang and the room held both of us in that moment, the crayon drawings on the wall and the afternoon light and a woman on the last edge of her life dancing to a song about being set free.","About halfway through the song she gently stilled. Her grip softened. She was asleep, or something close to it, still holding my hand.","I stayed until my tears cleared enough to drive. Then I said a prayer of thanks for what I had just witnessed and quietly let myself out.","Suzan never woke again. She drifted away peacefully a short time later, her family around her.","I have thought about that afternoon many times since. About what it means that music can reach a person when words cannot. About whatever it is that stays alive in us until the very end, still moving, still feeling, still capable of a smile that big.","About the small country church that shaped her faith. About the grandchildren on the wall who will never know their grandmother danced on her last afternoon. But whose drawings were there in the room when she did.","I do not have a theological explanation for what happened that day. I just know it was sacred.","And I know she knew every word."]},{"id":"s005","slug":"enlightenment","title":"Enlightenment","sub":"Sixty years of prayer to reach it. Now he wanted company for what was left of the walk.","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/enlightenment","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, Enlightenment · Read it on Grounded","mins":8,"preview":["Matthew is a Catholic monk in his eighties, a religious scholar who has spent decades in centered prayer, contemplation, and solitude. He came to this monastery at eighteen, fresh out of high school, and never left. He's been my hospice patient a few weeks now, one of five retired monks living out their days in a wing the order converted for hospice care, inside walls built for something older and holier.","He met me in the doorway, hunched hard over a cane built for a man a foot taller than him, one hand braced against the frame. But that smile of his took up more of his face than a frame that small should have allowed.","\"How are you feeling today, Matthew?\"","He didn't answer right away. \"Tired,\" he finally said. \"But blessed. Blessed for a man my age who still gets to complain about being tired.\"","He made his way to his chair cane first, then a hand on the armrest, then the rest of him, lowering like a man setting down something fragile that happened to be himself. I sat across from him, in the chair that was becoming mine.","Last time, he'd asked me something I'd only half answered. He wanted to experience a state of enlightenment when he died. He told me about a book, The Grace in Dying, and asked if I would walk with him through what it described. That was all he asked. He never asked me to read it myself."],"posted":"","topics":["dying","faith"]},{"id":"s006","slug":"grief-debt","title":"Grief Debt","sub":"","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/grief-debt","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, Grief Debt · Read it on Grounded","mins":4,"preview":["Some weeks just feel heavier than others.","This week I caught myself walking around numb. Not sad, not angry, just numb. That is usually my first clue that I have racked up too much grief debt.","Grief debt is what happens when life keeps handing you losses and you keep telling yourself you will process them later. The emotions pile up quietly like laundry you swear you are going to fold. Eventually the pile gets big enough that it starts tripping you when you walk through the room.","When the numbness shows up I have learned to run through a simple four step process that helps me start digging out.","First I notice it.","I just admit something is there. No fixing, no judgment, just awareness.","Second I name it."],"posted":"","topics":["grief"]},{"id":"s007","slug":"grounded-in-coffee","title":"Grounded in Coffee","sub":"","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":true,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/grounded-in-coffee","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, Grounded in Coffee · Read it on Grounded","mins":4,"preview":["The other day I was having one of those rough starts we all know too well. Everything already felt a little heavier than usual.","I was heading out on the highway to my first visit when a reckless driver came flying past and cut right in front of me. I slammed on the brakes. My work bag flew forward and knocked my full coffee cup across the seat. The outside of the car was fine. The inside? Total disaster, coffee everywhere.","Thanks for reading Grounded! Subscribe for free to receive new posts and support my work.","Of course, the same driver ended up stopped at the red light just ahead of me. I pulled up beside him and gave him my best stink eye. He rolled down his window and started yelling at me like it was my fault.","In that moment, I had to remind myself: You are a hospice chaplain on your way to care for patients and families. You cannot do this. I kept my eyes forward, left the window up, and let him speed away. But my whole body was shaking with adrenaline."],"posted":"","topics":[],"full":["The other day I was having one of those rough starts we all know too well. Everything already felt a little heavier than usual.","I was heading out on the highway to my first visit when a reckless driver came flying past and cut right in front of me. I slammed on the brakes. My work bag flew forward and knocked my full coffee cup across the seat. The outside of the car was fine. The inside? Total disaster, coffee everywhere.","Thanks for reading Grounded! Subscribe for free to receive new posts and support my work.","Of course, the same driver ended up stopped at the red light just ahead of me. I pulled up beside him and gave him my best stink eye. He rolled down his window and started yelling at me like it was my fault.","In that moment, I had to remind myself: You are a hospice chaplain on your way to care for patients and families. You cannot do this. I kept my eyes forward, left the window up, and let him speed away. But my whole body was shaking with adrenaline.","I had only a few minutes before my first visit, and I needed to show up calm, present, and actually useful to the family I was about to meet. So I pulled over near a quiet field, took a deep breath, and used the 5-4-3-2-1 grounding technique right there in the driver’s seat.","It goes like this:Five things you can see.Four things you can feel.Three things you can hear.Two things you can smell.One thing you can taste.","I named five things I could see: my work bag, the blue sky, the golden field, my hands gripping the wheel, and the spilled coffee on the floor.Four things I could feel: my feet on the floor mat, the fabric of my clothes, the seat against my back, and the warmth of the coffee still soaking into my pants.Three sounds: the hum of the engine, traffic whooshing by, and my own breathing finally beginning to slow.Two smells: spilled coffee and the crisp morning air coming through the cracked window.And finally, one taste: more of that coffee.","As I finished the exercise, I had to laugh out loud. I was officially grounded in coffee. Four out of five senses, all coffee.","That moment has stayed with me. In this work, and really in all of life, things spill. Plans fall apart. People cut us off (sometimes literally). Our bodies react before our minds can catch up. The question is always: how do we come back to center quickly so we can still show up for the people who need us?","Sometimes the tools are simple. Sometimes the mess itself becomes part of the medicine.","What about you? Have you ever had one of those mornings (or whole seasons) where everything felt like it was spilling over and you had to find your way back to grounded? I’d love to hear your stories in the comments.","If these kinds of real, everyday reflections on chaos, faith, grounding, and the messy beauty of life resonate with you, I’d be grateful if you subscribed. There are plenty more stories where this came from.","Thanks for reading."]},{"id":"s008","slug":"he-came-to-collect","title":"He Came to Collect","sub":"What a dying woman's refusal taught me about love","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/he-came-to-collect","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, He Came to Collect · Read it on Grounded","mins":6,"preview":["Evelyn looks nothing like someone who is dying.","She is bright-eyed, quick with a laugh, always dressed like she is expecting company. She joins card games in the sunroom, sings along at music hour, and keeps a running tally of who is winning at bingo. On paper she is on hospice. You would never know it.","I visit her every couple of weeks at the memory care facility that has become her home. To me she is the woman who grew up on a Midwest farm with three brothers and three sisters, who chased chickens and rode tractors with her dad and once found barn kittens in her school lunch pail. She is not religious in any formal way, but she loves talking about God the way a neighbor might speak of an old friend they have not seen in years but still think about fondly.","Some visits she will say, \"Chris, would you pray with me before you go?\" Other times she waves a hand and smiles. \"No thank you, dear. I am alright today.\" I never push. And though I know she has children, she never mentions them. I do not ask. In hospice you learn to sit inside the silences people offer you.","Then one Tuesday I poked my head into her room and saw a young man sitting stiffly in the visitor's chair.","He looked uneasy the moment he spotted me. Evelyn lit up.","\"Come in, Chris! This is my son, Derek.\"","Derek's jaw tightened. I offered my warmest smile and started to back out. \"I will let you two catch up.\"","\"No,\" Evelyn said quickly, almost sharply. \"Why don't you stay.\""],"posted":"","topics":["dying","kids"]},{"id":"s009","slug":"he-deserves-that","title":"He Deserves That","sub":"A sister's last phone call, and the room where the veil went thin","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/he-deserves-that","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, He Deserves That · Read it on Grounded","mins":10,"preview":["What started as a routine phone call quickly escalated into something else.","It was the end of my day and I was calling new admits to introduce myself and ask about spiritual care.","\"Hi Judy, my name is Chris. I'm one of the chaplains. Is now an okay time to talk?\"","She cut me off before I got the words out. \"Thank God! Bill is transitioning and you need to go see him right away before he dies!\"","While she spoke I was scrambling to pull up the chart and catch up on Bill's condition. I knew he was dying. He had just been admitted to hospice. I did not know we were this close.","\"I understand,\" I said. \"Bill is declining quickly, faster than expected. Judy, does Bill have a faith background or religious tradition I should be aware of?\"","\"I'm not really sure. We grew up Catholic, but Bill never really followed along with it when he got older. I know he still believes.\"","\"That makes sense. It's more common than you'd think. Do you think Bill would want to see a priest for Last Rites?\"","\"No, he was not much for priests or church. If you could do it, that would be better.\"","I was not sure exactly what she meant by that. I am not sure she knew exactly either. I just rode with it.","\"I can do that,\" I said. \"I'll head over now. I'll say some prayers with Bill, read some Scripture, and do my best to help him pass peacefully.\"","\"Thank you.\" Judy's voice broke open. \"That's what he needs. He's a good man. He deserves that.\""],"posted":"","topics":["dying","faith"]},{"id":"s010","slug":"he-was-praying-too","title":"He Was Praying Too","sub":"On miracles, doubt, and showing up anyway","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/he-was-praying-too","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, He Was Praying Too · Read it on Grounded","mins":10,"preview":["The pager went off right after dinner with my family.","I looked down: 66-year-old male, motorcycle accident, life flight.","I kissed my family goodbye and drove.","The charge nurse met me at the ED doors. \"Sounds bad. Unresponsive at the scene. CPR, epi, he has a pulse now but nonresponsive. Helicopter should be landing any minute. Family might be on the way.\"","\"Good thing you are here, Chaplain.\"","I headed to the lobby and scanned a roomful of worried faces. I did not even have a name yet. He was still a John Doe. I asked the desk if anyone had come in for a motorcycle accident. A young woman stood up immediately.","\"I am Sandy. My uncle Tom. Is he okay?\"","I took her to the family room, closed the door, offered tissues, and told her what little I knew. He was being life-flighted in. I would update her the moment I knew anything. Was there anyone she needed to call?","\"My mom. His sister.\" She pressed her hand over her mouth. \"He just got that stupid motorcycle. We all knew it was a bad idea.\"","I told her to call her mom and went to check on Tom.","The trauma team was already working on him in bay 4, cutting away bloody clothes, assessing injuries. The head injury was the thing. I looked at it and felt what I always feel in those moments: this is not survivable.","I found the neuro intensivist I had become friends with. He gave it to me straight. Tom had lost his pulse at the scene. Ten minutes of CPR before they got it back. He had coded again in the helicopter on the way in."],"posted":"","topics":["faith"]},{"id":"s012","slug":"if-she-is-still-here","title":"If She Is Still Here","sub":"I almost said tomorrow. She didn't have a tomorrow.","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/if-she-is-still-here","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, If She Is Still Here · Read it on Grounded","mins":7,"preview":["\"It's mom. She's taken a turn and not doing well. Can you come?\"","I was off that day, and my work phone was supposed to be off too. It never is. Not because I plan on answering everything that comes through at all hours, but in case a message like this one shows up, and I need to.","I had been visiting this family for about a year. Every visit started and ended with a hug, the kind that happens naturally once you've spent enough time with people to stop being a visitor.","I texted back: \"I'm sorry to hear this. I'm off today but I can come by tomorrow.\"","\"Okay,\" she replied. \"If she is still here.\"","My heart dropped. I'd been with Carol only a few days earlier, telling silly jokes, admiring her latest diamond art project. I had no idea she'd declined this fast.","I typed back fast. \"I can come now if that's okay?\"","\"Thank you.\" That was the only reply.","I grabbed my things and was out the door within minutes. Thirty minutes later I was standing at a front door I'd knocked on more than a dozen times.","The house was always the same. Grandkids' toys lined up neatly in the living room. Cozy furniture arranged to catch the light from the big window. Art on every wall, seasonal decor changed out like clockwork."],"posted":"","topics":["dying","grief"]},{"id":"s013","slug":"love","title":"Love","sub":"A Connection Words Could Never Carry","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/love","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, Love · Read it on Grounded","mins":7,"preview":["Kathy was glowing.","I noticed it from forty feet away, before I even reached her table.","Some mornings I get a pull toward a name on my caseload, and I can't explain it. No report, no reason. Just a need to go. That's what brought me to her memory care unit today.","\"Kathy! Hello! Good to see you!\" I turned on my biggest smile, the one I save for her.","Her dementia has taken most of her words. What's left, the charts call \"word salad.\" I've never liked that term.","I've been visiting Kathy for over a year now, long enough to learn how to hear her anyway. Before the dementia she was known for her quick wit and a laugh that filled a room.","She has outlived most of her family. Even her children are senior citizens now. She's lived here five years, since she could no longer care for herself.","I pulled a chair close. \"It's been a while. I was on vacation. How are you today, Kathy?\"","She wore a soft blue sweater. In her hands, a small blanket, no bigger than a washcloth, stitched like a quilt someone had made just for her. She rubbed it between her fingers as words spilled out in the wrong order.","Most people would hear nonsense. I listen for the one real word hiding inside the scatter. When I catch it, I say it back to her. She knows she's been heard.","Today she was more animated than I'd seen her in months. Her arms swung. Words scattered like marbles across a floor.","\"This is good…I am now…you are here...yes, thank you...do you have it?\""],"posted":"","topics":["faith"]},{"id":"s014","slug":"my-boundaries-have-gates","title":"My Boundaries Have Gates","sub":"","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/my-boundaries-have-gates","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, My Boundaries Have Gates · Read it on Grounded","mins":5,"preview":["Some mornings hit different before I even leave the house. That day started with a flood of messages, but one line stopped me cold: “Decline. Daily nursing visits. Family has been notified and is coping.”","I had to get there before it was too late.","On our previous visit, when I told him I’d see him again the next week, he looked at me with that half-joking, half-desperate smile and said, “You promise, right?”","I never make promises in this work. It’s one of my rules. But in that moment I held his hand, looked him in the eyes, and said yes anyway. This man has endured a brutally hard life with almost no one in his corner. I had grown genuinely fond of him. So I rearranged my whole morning and made him my first stop.","The facility smelled exactly like I remembered, thick and institutional. I found him half-propped in bed, legs dangling off the side, nothing but skin and bones, his face tight with pain. I tried adjusting him for comfort but this was as good as it was going to get. So I sat down, took his cold hands, and simply said hello.","He managed a small, surprised smile. “You came.”","“Of course I came,” I told him. “I made a promise.”","He gripped my hand tight. His body trembled with chills even as he said he felt hot. We kept swapping blankets on and off. His lips were dry and cracked. I offered water. He shook his head. That was okay too."],"posted":"","topics":["dying","grief"]},{"id":"s015","slug":"please-help-my-dad-die","title":"Please Help My Dad Die","sub":"Sometimes the hardest part of dying is finding permission to leave","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/please-help-my-dad-die","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, Please Help My Dad Die · Read it on Grounded","mins":7,"preview":["I was recently called to the bedside of a man who was actively dying. At admission to hospice, he had declined a chaplain outright. He wasn't religious, he said, and he wanted nothing to do with spiritual care. All I really knew was that he was struggling to let go, and his daughter hoped I might be able to help.","These moments come more often than you'd think. The details change, but the ache is familiar: someone hanging on, tethered by unfinished business. I never know quite what I'll find, so I simply show up, open, and listen.","I arrived at his daughter's well-kept home, filled with antiques and stories. It had been gently transformed into a makeshift hospice room. Hospital bed in the front living room. Walker and wheelchair tucked to the side. The ever-present bedside commode standing sentinel.","As his daughter led me to him, she shared their story in soft, halting sentences. Bob had been a successful lawyer, a self-made man who built a thriving practice now run by his eldest son. They were not a church-going family. He loved golf, loved the law, and for years that had been enough. Then his wife died, and something in him broke. He withdrew completely, disappearing into his work. \"We lost our mother,\" she said, \"and then we lost our father too.\"","\"So how did he come back?\" I asked gently."],"posted":"","topics":["dying","kids"]},{"id":"s016","slug":"prayer","title":"Prayer","sub":"A Language Dementia Cannot Touch","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/thank-god-for-sending-you","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, Prayer · Read it on Grounded","mins":10,"preview":["Trash caught in the shrubs by the entrance. My heart sank a little before I even reached the door.","Inside, the lobby smelled like urine. I hadn't even reached the unit yet.","I sat by a small glass window and waited for someone to buzz me in. A young staff member finally came, read my badge, and gave me a short, flat look. She didn't say anything. She didn't have to.","I found the locked door at the end of the hall, entered the code, and the lock clicked open.","The day room was full. Wheelchairs everywhere, some cradling baby dolls, others stuffed animals held like they were real. A small group played a game at a table with the only staff member I could find. I didn't see my patient anywhere, so I walked over and asked for help.","He looked up, annoyed I'd interrupted the game. I understood. One staff member, a room full of people who all needed something. I introduced myself and asked about Dee.","He scanned the room. \"Dee?\" Then, \"I don't see her.\"","\"Is she missing?\"","\"No. She probably went back to her room.\"","We walked there together. Empty. I watched panic cross his face for the first time. He shot past me toward the nurses' station, said something to the staff there, then fast walked across the day room to a small side activity room. I followed.","Dee sat alone in her wheelchair, folding a napkin back and forth in her lap.","\"Dee! How did you get over here?\" the staff member said, half laughing, wheeling her out. \"You aren't supposed to do that.\""],"posted":"","topics":["faith"]},{"id":"s017","slug":"the-atypical-atheist","title":"The Atypical Atheist","sub":"How one visit broke through years of religious distrust","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":true,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/the-atypical-atheist","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, The Atypical Atheist · Read it on Grounded","mins":4,"preview":["I missed the note. Classic me.","I walked into the group home ready for a visit and the man took one look at my badge and exploded. \"I don't want a damn chaplain. I don't believe in any of that bs.\"","I smiled, hands open and calm. \"Totally fine. We don't have to talk about any of that bs if you don't want to. So tell me, what do you believe in?\"","He stared, caught completely off guard. Then he cracked a grin. \"I didn't think chaplains were allowed to cuss.\"","\"I am not your average chaplain. Give me a chance. I might surprise you.\"","We started with easy guy talk. Sports, the weather, the sheer absurdity of how fast life moves when you are not looking. Then he locked eyes with me and dropped the act.","\"Enough beating around the bush. I know why you are here.\"","I honestly did not know yet. So I asked.","He leaned in, voice dropping. \"Look, I tell people I am an atheist because it keeps the religious people away. I cannot stand their hypocrisy. But honestly? I am not really an atheist. I believe in God, or something. I just cannot stand the church crap.\""],"posted":"","topics":["faith"],"full":["I missed the note. Classic me.","I walked into the group home ready for a visit and the man took one look at my badge and exploded. \"I don't want a damn chaplain. I don't believe in any of that bs.\"","I smiled, hands open and calm. \"Totally fine. We don't have to talk about any of that bs if you don't want to. So tell me, what do you believe in?\"","He stared, caught completely off guard. Then he cracked a grin. \"I didn't think chaplains were allowed to cuss.\"","\"I am not your average chaplain. Give me a chance. I might surprise you.\"","We started with easy guy talk. Sports, the weather, the sheer absurdity of how fast life moves when you are not looking. Then he locked eyes with me and dropped the act.","\"Enough beating around the bush. I know why you are here.\"","I honestly did not know yet. So I asked.","He leaned in, voice dropping. \"Look, I tell people I am an atheist because it keeps the religious people away. I cannot stand their hypocrisy. But honestly? I am not really an atheist. I believe in God, or something. I just cannot stand the church crap.\"","I laughed warmly. \"I am not a huge fan of the institution myself.\"","His eyebrows shot up. I told him I take the Bible seriously when it says we are the church. Not the building. Not the brand. The people.","He lit up like a man who had finally been understood.","We talked real talk about faith, doubt, and the messiness of organized religion. How it can wound even as it tries to heal. He shared stories of past hurts that still stung. I shared a few of my own.","He told me about a pastor who had visited his father in the hospital years ago, said a quick prayer, and left without ever asking his father's name. \"That was the moment,\" he said quietly. \"I decided the whole thing was a performance.\"","I nodded. I told him about my own complicated relationship with organized religion. How I had watched institutions protect themselves instead of people. How I had sat with enough dying men and women to know that whoever God is, God is not impressed by buildings or budgets.","He was quiet for a moment. Then: \"So why are you still doing this?\"","\"Because of rooms like this one,\" I said. \"Because of conversations like this one.\"","He looked at the floor for a long time.","Then out of nowhere he asked me to pray for him.","\"What for?\" I asked gently.","\"That I will go to Heaven when I die.\"","We prayed together. Simple, honest, raw.","When I finished he was in tears, shoulders shaking with relief.","I walked to my car stunned and sat there in the quiet for a long moment. Some of the deepest spiritual conversations at the end of life happen not when we push religion but when we stop pushing altogether and simply meet people where they are, in all their beautiful contradictions and hidden longing."]},{"id":"s019","slug":"the-impossible-dance-of-particles","title":"The Impossible Dance of Particles","sub":"A brilliant physicist rediscovers faith through her microscope","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/the-impossible-dance-of-particles","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, The Impossible Dance of Particles · Read it on Grounded","mins":3,"preview":["I met her in a quiet corner of the nursing home. The staff adored her, but when hospice arrived she immediately waved off any offer to see a chaplain. “Not religious,” she said with finality.","Fair enough.","Then the difficult days came. Pain clawed at her body and the reality of her situation pressed in. A nurse gently asked again if she would talk to someone. “Just to talk,” she said. “No religion.” The woman sighed deeply and agreed.","I walked in expecting the classic skeptic, sharp and closed off. Instead I met a brilliant physicist whose research had taken her around the world. No children. Never married. She had lived alone most of her life, yet the staff clearly loved her like family.","When I sat down she looked at me with tired but curious eyes and said, “I believe in God actually. Just not the church version.”"],"posted":"","topics":["faith"]},{"id":"s020","slug":"the-recovery","title":"The Recovery","sub":"What happened after mattered more than the mistake.","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":true,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/the-recovery","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, The Recovery · Read it on Grounded","mins":11,"preview":["I broke a dying woman's saucer yesterday. It is not the breaking that stays with me. It is everything that happened after.","Gail lives in a locked memory care unit, in a small room with one window, a big TV, a hospital bed, and a china cabinet. The walls are mostly bare, just a few photographs. I have been visiting her for a few months now. She is easygoing, keeps mostly to herself, and her dementia makes real conversation more work than reward some days, more frustration than connection. Early on, our visits were quiet. I would sit near her recliner, she would drift, and I never knew exactly where I stood with her.","Then one afternoon I noticed the china cabinet.","It held a small collection of antique cups and saucers, and I asked her about it, mostly to fill the silence. Something in Gail opened like a window.","\"I got this one when I was just a young girl. With my grandmother.\" She pointed to another. \"This one here, my husband gave me.\"","\"Is he passed now? What was his name?\"","\"Yah. Several years back now. Jim.\"","I have learned since that Gail cannot always tell you what she had for breakfast. But she can tell you the story behind every piece in that cabinet, where it came from, who gave it to her, what year. It is as though the cups and saucers hold the memories her mind cannot anymore. We look at them together most visits now, her eyes fixed on the glass, gone thirty, forty, sixty years, telling me who she used to be.","Yesterday I walked in and asked how she was doing."],"posted":"","topics":[],"full":["I broke a dying woman's saucer yesterday. It is not the breaking that stays with me. It is everything that happened after.","Gail lives in a locked memory care unit, in a small room with one window, a big TV, a hospital bed, and a china cabinet. The walls are mostly bare, just a few photographs. I have been visiting her for a few months now. She is easygoing, keeps mostly to herself, and her dementia makes real conversation more work than reward some days, more frustration than connection. Early on, our visits were quiet. I would sit near her recliner, she would drift, and I never knew exactly where I stood with her.","Then one afternoon I noticed the china cabinet.","It held a small collection of antique cups and saucers, and I asked her about it, mostly to fill the silence. Something in Gail opened like a window.","\"I got this one when I was just a young girl. With my grandmother.\" She pointed to another. \"This one here, my husband gave me.\"","\"Is he passed now? What was his name?\"","\"Yah. Several years back now. Jim.\"","I have learned since that Gail cannot always tell you what she had for breakfast. But she can tell you the story behind every piece in that cabinet, where it came from, who gave it to her, what year. It is as though the cups and saucers hold the memories her mind cannot anymore. We look at them together most visits now, her eyes fixed on the glass, gone thirty, forty, sixty years, telling me who she used to be.","Yesterday I walked in and asked how she was doing.","\"Been better,\" she said, from her recliner. Not feeling well. Hard day. But she had news. A new set had arrived, and she wanted to show me before I even sat down.","\"Hey, I gotta show ya dat new cup and set I got!\"","She pointed to a small teacup and saucer, cream colored with gold trim. \"Ya can't find 'em like dat anymore,\" she said. \"Not easy, anyway.\"","I picked it up, turning it in the light. \"Gail, this is beautiful. Was it a gift?\"","That is when I saw it. A small bead of dried glue at the base, holding the saucer to the cup.","\"I think these are glued together,\" I said.","\"No, I don't tink so.\"","I lifted the cup to show her, gently, saucer still stuck beneath it.","I watched her face change first. Something crossed it, fast, before I understood why. Then the saucer let go.","Time did something strange in that half second. I remember both of us shouting. I remember reaching for it. I remember missing.","It hit the floor and did not crack. It exploded. Twenty, maybe thirty pieces, scattered across the room.","My heart actually stopped, I think, for a second. This woman is dying. She had just shown me the one good thing that had happened to her that day, and I destroyed it in front of her.","Nobody trains you for this part of the job. They train you to be careful, to move slow around fragile, breakable things. Nobody trains you for the ten seconds after you were not careful enough. I knew right then this visit was going to go one of two ways. I was either about to get uninvited from Gail's room for good, or I was about to give the performance of my career.","I dropped to my knees in front of her. I let out a groan I did not plan and put my head down like a man in front of a judge.","\"Gail. I am so sorry. I feel terrible. I cannot believe I just did that.\"","She looked down at me, and instead of anger, I got grace.","\"Oh, shit happens,\" she said. \"Had someone break a real special saucer on me once. Didn't even say sorry. At least you did.\"","I started gathering pieces off the floor while my stomach kept sinking with every new shard I found.","\"Well,\" Gail said, \"maybe we glue it back. I'm pretty good at the gluein'. Ya got all the pieces?\"","She was not just forgiving me. She was already fixing it with me.","I ended up flat on my belly, army crawling under her hospital bed with my phone flashlight, calling up from underneath, \"I think I got most of 'em, Gail, but they shattered pretty bad. I'm real sorry.\"","She laughed. A real one, from the belly, watching a grown man in a chaplain badge crawl around her floor looking for teacup shrapnel. Gail has a crass, sideways sense of humor, and I think this was the most entertained she had been all week.","I decided to lean into it. \"God, what kind of chaplain comes into your room and breaks your stuff?\"","She grinned. \"I know, right. He's supposed to be helpin' me and instead he's in here breakin' things.\"","We went back and forth like that for a minute, both of us laughing at me now, and I could not believe how fast this had turned. I got serious again for a second.","\"Gail, I really am sorry. I have to admit, I was a clumsy kid. This kind of thing happened a lot.\"","\"Well,\" she said, \"some tings never change.\"","Her eyes were glassy, but happy glassy. I told her I owed her, big time, and asked what I could do to make it right.","\"Ya don't hafta do nothin',\" she said. \"Just don't break nothin' else, okay?\"","I offered chocolate. She was not interested. I offered flowers, and something shifted in her face.","\"Yah,\" she said slowly. \"Some fresh flowers would be nice.\"","\"Any favorites? Lilies, roses?\"","\"Oh, I like 'em all. Don't need to be fancy.\"","Before I left I asked if I could pray with her, and she said yes, of course, please. I prayed for her comfort, for good days ahead, and I asked God, quietly, to help me make this right and do better by her. Gail snickered partway through. We laughed together after the amen, the kind of laugh that only makes sense if you were in the room.","I stopped at the nursing station on my way out, held up the little bag of shards, and told the story. The nurse laughed so hard she had to sit down, and wished me luck on the repair.","When I left, Gail was laughing, glowing even. Further from the hard day she had been having than when I arrived. A broken saucer had somehow become the best part of her afternoon.","That night I glued what I could, piece by piece, at my kitchen table. Two small fragments, about the size of baby teeth, are still lost somewhere near her bed. I looked up the pattern. English bone china, 1936. Gail was right, you cannot walk into a store and buy one of these anymore.","But you can find almost anything if you look long enough, and by the end of the night I had won a nearly identical one at auction.","In a few days I will walk back into that small room with the one window, a saucer in one hand and a bouquet in the other. Gail keeps her whole life inside that cabinet now, since her mind will not hold it for her anymore. I do not know which memory she will reach for when she looks at this one. I do not think she will remember the break for long, if she remembers it at all. But I think she will remember what happened after, the apology, the floor, the laughing. That is the part that stays, I have come to believe. Not the mistake. The recovery."]},{"id":"s021","slug":"total-bliss","title":"Total Bliss","sub":"Not afraid. Not even close. Just eager.","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/total-bliss","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, Total Bliss · Read it on Grounded","mins":5,"preview":["\"How do you handle all the death and dying?\"","People ask me this more than anything else. Young chaplain me used to wonder the same thing, quietly, on the drive home some nights. I have an answer now, and it still catches people off guard: most of the deaths I witness are not the tragedy everyone braces for. There is no wailing. No suffering. There is reminiscing, life's most cherished moments told one more time. There is gratitude, the kind that surprises even the person feeling it. There is a closeness to God I have never found anywhere else, right where the veil between life and death goes thin.","Jane taught me that better than almost anyone.","I met her in an assisted living facility a few days before she died. Her room was small and full of her, photographs crowding every flat surface, a quilt someone had clearly made by hand folded at the foot of the bed. Afternoon light came through the blinds in stripes across the covers. She had been a young widow, left to raise two children alone after her husband Gary died overseas. She told me how, in the worst of that grief, God had promised her He would take care of her and her children.","\"Did you ever remarry?\" I asked."],"posted":"","topics":["dying"]},{"id":"s022","slug":"welcome-home","title":"Welcome Home","sub":"On ancestors, thresholds, and the ones who come to walk us home.","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/welcome-home","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, Welcome Home · Read it on Grounded","mins":6,"preview":["\"Dad is seeing ghosts. What does this mean?\"","The text came in before I'd taken my first sip of coffee, and I already knew what it meant. I didn't want to be the one to say it over text, so I wrote back, \"We might be getting close. Can I come by?\"","\"Yes.\"","I drove out to the trailer park by the river, the one with no trees and no flowers in the yards, and parked next to Jim's old Ford, the one that hadn't moved in years. I let myself in through the screened patio he built with his own hands, the same patio where he used to tell me about his life. Builder. Mechanic. Digger. Truck driver. Electrician. I lost count of the jobs somewhere around six. A man who could fix anything except what was happening to him now.","Today he wasn't on the patio. He was in a hospital bed crammed into the small living room,no covers on him, a fan blowing across his skin, and still he was sweating.","Julie was standing over him when I came in. She didn't say hello. She just stared at her dad like she was trying to memorize him.","\"How's he doing?\" I asked, resting a hand on his shoulder. Heat came off him like a stove left on.","\"Okay now. We gave him something for pain, so he's sleeping.\"","\"Any food or water today?\"","\"No. Not since yesterday. He had a few sips before all this started.\" She threw her arms wide when she said it, taking in the whole room.","\"All of this?\" I asked, echoing the gesture."],"posted":"","topics":["dying"]},{"id":"s085","slug":"off-duty","title":"Off Duty","sub":"Hospice is different when it's one of ours.","col":"New Stories","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, from my notebook. Names and details changed.","mins":7,"preview":["David wasn't wearing teal.","In almost two years on the same team, I had never seen him out of scrubs. Now he sat in the visitor's chair in a gray hoodie, elbows on his knees, car keys still in his fist like he hadn't decided whether to stay.","His father lay in the bed by the window, turned toward the glass. He didn't look at me. He didn't look at anything.","David stood up out of habit and gave me report.","\"Left-sided weakness from the stroke. Eating a few bites a day. Refusing meds. Refusing showers. Skin's holding, for now.\"","Then he heard himself and sat back down.","\"Sorry. That's my dad.\"","Most families give me a story. David gave me a chart.","He's the nurse families ask for by name. I've watched him walk into a room full of dread and calm it just by pulling up a chair. Today he couldn't find a chair that fit.","\"Hi, Don,\" I said. \"I'm Chris, the chaplain.\"","Nothing.","\"Six weeks,\" David said. \"Not a word. Not to me. Not to anybody.\"","On the dresser sat a few photos, a faded VA ball cap, and a coffee mug that said WORLD'S OKAYEST GRANDPA.","\"You know what's crazy?\" David said. \"I say hospice forty times a day. I can't say it about my own dad. It keeps coming out as 'a little extra support.'\"","I knew exactly what he meant. We talk about death all day long. Then it's one of ours, and none of the words fit.","\"Tell me about him,\" I said. \"Not the chart. Him.\"","For the first time, he smiled."],"posted":"","topics":["dying","kids"]}],"lock":{"iv":"SH1RJtnltmjmkImX","ct":""},"letters":[],"cards":[],"ws":"kJv6Og3Omdt+fOhNNDoftA==","wraps":[{"c":"miepkvo","salt":"rM/o7A/oC4eyCadoQ4Eeyg==","iv":"/BLCkYBFfwVpi3EE","ct":"6SEBTnKVV2M/b7iPYOs83pZmV5fnN4CE+OKUwTTcERMcfB7hQMGCuaOEE8fTi9zi"},{"c":"mt1r3d8","iv":"+osOWdg9Z/e6ispw","ct":"aTLwqtt4NIuU1A7Z1ch4L/hj/fEfCnXa+Zm+3EQ+6kO4VDePYfSq25zr+jf4Afoq"},{"c":"m6n617b","iv":"VddfSSD4Usp8pqGF","ct":"RMNTjw2TZklJteKkg0fUcdocBF1rWqaZSKh7WeFQhbjz0cmcj6NSJ1CbFoLPgfGo"}]};
